  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery with 500mb heights overlaid shows the axis of \nthe upper ridge roughly along or just east of the Rockies. This \nplaces the middle to lower Missouri Valley within northwesterly flow \naloft. At the surface, we're in a bit of a pressure col with high \npressure building into the Great Lakes. This surface high will\nbecome more established overnight and allow for weak easterly \nto southeasterly low-level winds to develop. This sets up an \narea of low to mid-level convergence from north-central KS into \nsoutheastern MO and it's this area of convergence that will \nhelp lead to an area of showers and storms developing overnight \ninto tomorrow morning. Through the overnight, isentropic ascent,\naided by the low to mid- level convergence, develops adding \nsome forcing needed for precipitation. Severe weather is not \nexpected with this activity. The forecast area remains at that \ninterface of the high pressure to our east and westerly mid-\nlevel flow over the Plains. This keeps an area of convergence \nand isentropic ascent situated across eastern KS and western MO \nthrough Thursday, leading to multiple chances for showers and \nstorms in the area. Severe weather chances remain low through \nThursday with relatively weak flow aloft limiting shear. \n\nA stronger shortwave trough will move across the Northern \nPlains/ Upper Midwest Thursday night - Friday. This will help \npush a cold front through the area and lead to the best chances \nfor precipitation through this forecast period. The threat of \nsevere weather continues to look low, with marginal shear and \ninstability present. But PWAT values over 2\" and strong moisture\ntransport into the area, locally heavy rainfall looks possible.\n\nIn the wake of this system, cooler and drier air will move into the \narea for the weekend. Highs should top out in the low to mid 80s but \nafter a week of dewpoints in the middle 70s to lower 80s, dewpoints \nin the low to mid 60s will be noticeably drier.",
